// Sample chapter (session 4): a short chapter that uses every player feature, so the codex, the
// People screen, the family tree, content notes, achievements, rewind and the look of the reader
// can all be tested at once. Developer book only; it is not part of the story.
@chapter etd1
@title Sample Chapter: The Test Hill
@book engine-test-d
@version 1

# start
## Dawn on the Test Hill
The road climbs out of the sleeping city in the last of the dark. {name} walks it alone, as {they} {have} walked a hundred like it, and the first grey of morning finds the hilltop before {they} arrive{s}.
Below, the city is still lit: thousands of small lamps, each one a room where someone is not yet ready to sleep. The sky behind the far ridge turns the color of old brass.
> *The best view of any city is the one you see just before you must leave it.*
$ do discover("et_place")
$ do meet("et_love")
?if not in_circle("et_keeper")
  $ do keeper("et_keeper", "steward")
?end
---
Someone has been here before you. A cloak lies folded on the wall, and beside it a note in a careful hand. It says only that the morning belongs to those who are patient with it.
:the_hill
* Wait for the sun on the hill
  $ flag et.mark
  $ et.hill = "stayed"
  The light climbs the ridge a finger's width at a time. You are in no hurry. You have never been in a hurry, not in all the years you can count.
  $ do discover("et_lore")
* Go down and find who left the cloak
  $ et.hill = "climbed"
  You take the cloak with you and follow the road back toward the lamps.
-> family

# family
$ do kin("hero")
$ do kin("et_child")
$ do kin("et_grandchild")
## Those who came after
The names come back to you in the order you taught them to others: first the child, then the child's child, both of whom you would gladly have kept in sight a little longer.
:the_second_choice
* Remember them kindly
  $ do awaken("general", "hero")
  Your memory is gentler than the years were. Somewhere in the same hour, an old and patient thing opens its eyes.
* Remember them as they were
  $ do kin("et_grandchild", "dead")
  Memory does not soften for you. You hold each of them exactly as they stood.
---
-> ending

# ending
## Morning
?if et.mark
  Because you waited, the whole hill is gold, and you see the cloak's owner standing at the edge of the road, watching you with a smile.
?else
  You are halfway down when the sun finally clears the ridge, and the lamps go out street by street as though a hand were passing over them.
?end
The Test Hill keeps its secrets. The day begins, and you are, for now, only a traveler on the road.
=> endbook
